# Performance — fast by default, nothing extra

Budgets (engineering targets, not spec text), mid-range phone on 4G: LCP < 2.5 s cold / < 1 s repeat, INP < 200 ms, A1 first-load JS ≤ 150 KB gz, hot query p95 < 100 ms.

## Code

- Server Components for first paint. Client JS only for composer, long-press sheet, camera, sliders.
- A1 ships no calendar, camera, album, or ads code — `import()` those screens.
- Hot writes (send, react, mark-read) go browser → Supabase under RLS. No extra Next API hop. Privileged work (admit, verify, album password) goes through the server.
- One round trip per screen: one query/RPC or `Promise.all`. Never await in a loop.
- Optimistic send and react. Debounce search 150–300 ms. Long lists: CSS `content-visibility: auto` first; virtualize only if measured.
- `proxy.ts` (Next 16's middleware) only refreshes the session — no DB queries in it.
- Membership check once per request: DAL functions in `lib/auth/dal.ts` wrapped in React `cache()`, shared by layout and page. Verify with `getClaims()` (local JWT check), not `getUser()` (network call) or `getSession()` (unverified).
- `cacheComponents` is on: read cookies/params/DB inside a `<Suspense>` with a skeleton so the static shell paints first.
- System font stack (zero download). `next/image` for static assets only. No new dependency — see the allowlist in `project-structure.mdc`.

## Database

- Same region for DB and server: Supabase `eu-north-1` (Stockholm) + Vercel functions `arn1` (Stockholm). A cross-region hop adds latency to every query and keeps data in the EU.
- Every list query: `community_id` (or chat membership) + explicit columns + `limit`. No `select('*')`; no `count: 'exact'` on hot paths.
- Messages: per-chat `seq bigint`. Page with keyset `where chat_id = $1 and seq < $cursor order by seq desc limit 30`. Index `(chat_id, seq)`. Never OFFSET.
- Unread: `chats.last_seq` (one write per message) and `memberships.last_read_seq`. Unread = `last_seq - last_read_seq` in the A1 select. Never `COUNT(*)`; never update every member's row per message (a 460-member group would mean 460 writes).
- Mark-read: write when the chat is opened and when the user leaves it, not once per incoming message.
- Other indexes: `(community_id)`, `(user_id, community_id)` on memberships, `(community_id, last_message_at desc)` for A1.
- RLS: filter on indexed columns; write `(select auth.uid())`, not bare `auth.uid()`.
- Dynamic fields: one row per value with typed columns (`value_text`, `value_num`, `value_bool`). Multi-select = one row per chosen option. Index `(field_def_id, value_num)` and `(field_def_id, value_text)`. Search runs in one RPC with `limit`.

## Realtime

- At most two channels per client: one per-user channel (A1 bumps) and one for the open chat.
- Push new messages with **Broadcast** (sent on insert). Don't use `postgres_changes` for chat — it re-checks RLS for every subscriber and slows down big groups.

## Storage

- Resize on the client before upload: max ~1600 px plus a ~256 px thumbnail. Encode WebP; if `blob.type` isn't `image/webp` (older Safari falls back to PNG), use JPEG q≈0.8. Re-encoding also strips EXIF.
- Lists load thumbnails; the full image only on tap.
- Immutable paths `{community}/{user}/{uuid}.webp` + long `cache-control`. Replace = new path.
- Private bucket for chat images, albums, and verification photos. Sign in batch (`createSignedUrls`) and reuse each URL until it expires. Album URLs only after the server password check.
- Server enforces type + max size. The limit is TBD — keep it in one constant.

## PWA

- `app/manifest.ts` + `public/sw.js` with **no fetch handler**. Hashed `/_next/static/*` is already immutable in the HTTP cache; a fetch handler would make every navigation wait for the worker to boot.
- Do not cache HTML, RSC payloads, API, or messages — they're authenticated and live, and offline mode is not decided.

## Don't overdo

No Redis, microservices, GraphQL, global state store, offline sync engine, or custom CDN until a measured bottleneck calls for it. Measure first (Lighthouse, `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`). For what to add when something breaks, and what may be stale, see `scaling.mdc`.
